**About us:**

The Ombuds Office for SOS Children's Villages is an independent entity within SOS Children’s Villages dedicated to supporting individuals concerned about the safety or well-being of children and young people involved in SOS programmes and services, past or present. Its approach is child-centred, participatory, and 'ground-up', meaning it adapts to each national context and seeks local solutions to local challenges.

An Ombuds is a trustworthy individual who listens, supports, and guides children, young people, and adults to find solutions to their concerns related to children and young people.

Each Ombuds follows the four principles: Confidentiality, Impartiality, Independence and Informality. They work independently of SOS Children’s Villages as a check and balance to safeguarding with the goal of finding a solution to the concern raised.

The Angola National Association of SOS Children’s Villages is an organization dedicated to caring for and protecting children and young people in vulnerable situations by providing them with a loving family environment. We are committed to child safeguarding and seek a highly qualified and dedicated professional to join our team in the position of **National Ombuds**.

**About the role:**

Location: Angola/Lubango

*Fulltime Employment contract – 1 year, with the possibility of renewing, based on performance and existing funds.*

To further their mission, SOS Children’s Villages has decided to implement an Ombuds Office.

The National Ombuds Angola, as designated neutral professionals, will build awareness, promote prevention, nurture child participation, and enable children and young people to be safe in all SOS CV programs and services.

She/He is a person of trust who has the skills and interest to work through their concerns and find a local solution by:

- Adhering to the principles of confidentiality, independence, informality and impartiality;
- Guiding a fair process through listening and supporting;
- Utilizing dispute resolution skills;
- Being a check and balance;
- Identifying trends and making recommendations to SOS Children’s Villages of Angola.

***The National Ombuds will operate independently from SOS Children’s Villages Angola and will report to the Regional Ombuds***.

**Profile, skills and qualifications:**

- Minimum 5 years demonstrated experience in one of the following fields: ombudsperson, mediation, child safeguarding, child protection, or child rights;
- Bachelor’s or higher degree in a relevant area such as child rights or child protection, child and youth studies, mediation, social work, child psychology, safeguarding or psychosocial wellbeing, law, or equivalent;
- Good level of knowledge of children’s rights or child protection/prevention and related issues - child safeguarding, child development and health, law, policies, regulations and International Standards related to children's care;
- Minimum 3 years demonstrated experience in working with children and youths;
- Experience in working in communities;
- Very good listener with good observational skills;
- Strong communication skills;
- Strong facilitation/training skills - Ability to develop and implement engaging trainings and ongoing support workshops for staff and children' representatives;
- Demonstrated ability to build rapport with children and young people, to identify marginalized children and young people and connect with them and build trust;
- A problem solver, adviser and mediator capable of resolving young people’s and adults’ concerns effectively and in a timely manner;
- Understanding of the local context, including laws, policies, practices, language and social norms;
- Be gender and diverse sensitive and representative;
- Portuguese and basic English skills are mandatory.
- Understand the foundations of the Ombuds’ work;
- You have the legal right to live and work in Angola;
- Experience working in an NGO or international organization would be an asset.

**We offer**:

- Training and development
- Support from the Regional Ombuds
- Satisfying and stimulating work
- Remote and in-person work arrangements
- The opportunity to work for an NGO that is a member of a recognized international network.

In accordance with the organization’s child protection policy, this position will be subject to applicable background checks, including criminal record checks.

Under the overall supervision of the Chief of Mission (CoM) in Abuja and the direct supervision of the Head of Migration Management Unit, the Programme Manager (Immigration and Border Governance, Migration Governance, and Labour Mobility) will be responsible and accountable for managing and providing professional technical and programme support to the International Organization for Migration (IOM) Nigeria’s work on immigration and border governance, migration governance, and labour mobility.

The Programme Manager will contribute to the development and implementation of programmes, policy engagement, stakeholder coordination, research and capacity-building activities within the assigned areas. The Programme Manager will work closely with relevant IOM units, field offices, the Regional Office (RO) and Headquarters (HQ), as well as Government counterparts, United Nations (UN) agencies, donors, development partners, civil society, private sector and other stakeholders.

The Programme Manager will also contribute to ensuring that relevant interventions are aligned with IOM policies and strategies, national priorities, and applicable regional and global frameworks.

**Responsibilities**

1. Contribute in advancing the implementation, monitoring, and reporting of the Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration (GCM), through engagement with Government, United Nations (UN) entities, and development partners.
2. Manage the activities of the UN Migration Network in IOM Nigeria and facilitate inter-agency collaboration, joint planning, and information-sharing that aligns collective action on migration governance priorities.
3. Develop, review, and monitor migration governance frameworks, policies, strategies, and action plans at national and sub-national levels and provide technical advice that improves their coherence and implementation.
4. Contribute to the integration of migration considerations into national development frameworks and UN processes, including the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), Common Country Analysis (CCA), and United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF) to position migration as a cross-cutting development priority.
5. Provide technical inputs to policy dialogue, advocacy initiatives, institutional strengthening efforts, and migration governance programming to shape stakeholder positions and inform decisions of senior counterparts.
6. Develop and implement programmes and initiatives promoting safe, regular and well-managed labour migration and mobility to deliver results aligned with national priorities and donor expectations.
7. Provide technical guidance on labour migration governance, skills mobility, ethical recruitment, and protection of migrant workers to strengthen institutional approaches and safeguards.
8. Formulate policy recommendations, research, and capacity-building initiatives on labour migration and mobility, engaging stakeholders related to build consensus and sustain their commitment.
9. Manage the implementation of Immigration and Border Governance (IBG) projects and initiatives, including regular pathways, legal identity, border management, readmission cooperation, counter-migrant smuggling, and humanitarian border management to deliver results within agreed timelines, budgets and quality standards.
10. Contribute technical inputs to policy development, operational tools and institutional strengthening initiatives to enhance safe, orderly and regular migration pathways.
11. Strengthen resource mobilisation, project development and donor engagement relating to IBG priorities in coordination with relevant IOM units and technical specialists, expanding the funding base for the portfolio.
12. Manage, develop and contribute to the design of projects, including concept notes, proposals, budgets, workplans and results frameworks that are aligned with the Country Office (CO) strategic priorities.
13. Manage programme implementation and performance against agreed targets and apply corrective measures, upholding IOM policies, donor requirements and established quality standards.

**Required Qualifications and Experience**

**Education**

- Master’s degree in Political Science, International Relations, Migration Studies, Public Policy, Public Administration, Development Studies, Law, Economics, Social Sciences, or a related field from an accredited academic institution with five years of relevant professional experience; or,
- University degree in the above fields with seven years of relevant professional experience.

Accredited Universities are those listed in the UNESCO World Higher Education Database.

**Experience**

- Professional experience in migration governance, migration policy, protection, labour migration and mobility, migration management or a related field;
- Experience supporting the development and implementation of programmes and projects,
preferably in the migration or development context;
- Experience in policy analysis, research, stakeholder engagement and/or policy dialogue;
- Experience working with Government institutions, UN agencies, international organisations, donors, civil society, private sector or other relevant stakeholders;
- Experience in migration governance, labour migration and mobility, or protection-related
programming is desirable;
- Experience contributing to the development of concept notes, project proposals, reports, policy briefs and other programme or knowledge products;
- Experience supporting consultations, technical working groups, workshops, training or other multi-stakeholder activities;
- Experience in programme monitoring, reporting and documentation; and,
- Experience working in an international organisation, UN agency, government institution or development/humanitarian organisation is an advantage.

**Skills**

- Knowledge of migration-related issues and institutional frameworks in Nigeria and/or West and Central Africa is an advantage;
- Excellent knowledge of migration governance, migration policy, protection, and labour migration and mobility;
- Excellent programme and project management skills;
- Demonstrated analytical, research and problem-solving skills;
- Excellent stakeholder engagement, coordination and communication skills;
- Ability to prepare clear and concise reports, policy briefs, concept notes, presentations and other technical documents;
- Ability to work effectively with Government counterparts, development partners and other stakeholders;
- Ability to contribute to programme development, resource mobilisation, and partnership-building activities;
- Excellent organisational skills and ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines; and,
- Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications.

**Languages**

For this position, fluency in English is required (oral and written).

Working knowledge of another official UN languages (Arabic, Chinese, French, Russian, and Spanish) is an advantage.

Proficiency of language(s) required will be specifically evaluated during the selection process, which may include written and/or oral assessments.

**Background**

MSF UK has experienced continued growth and increasing organisational complexity, creating a need to ensure that its payroll arrangements remain effective, resilient and appropriate for its current and future requirements.

Payroll is currently delivered through a combination of internal processes and an outsourced payroll provider. MSF UK’s existing operating model includes several manual processes, interventions and duplicated checks across internal teams and the outsourced payroll provider. While these arrangements continue to support the delivery of payroll, they create inefficiencies, reliance on individual knowledge and exacerbate the inherent risk of human error.

There is also a business continuity risk associated with the current operating model including key person dependency and a single point of failure. Interim measures have been put in place to mitigate immediate operational risk; however these do not represent a sustainable long-term solution.

Accordingly, a decision has been made to undertake an independent review of MSF UK’s end-to-end payroll function to determine the most appropriate future operating model. This will include consideration of process efficiency, systems and technology, roles and responsibilities, governance and controls, organisational ownership, resourcing and the relationship with the outsourced payroll provider.

MSF UK is seeking an experienced payroll consultant to undertake an independent, evidence-based review of the current operating model and provide recommendations for a future operating model, which is robust, efficient and scalable.

Additional details of MSF UK’s payroll process relevant for this consultancy:

MSF UK run two monthly payroll runs, being:

1. **OFFICE**: Includes mostly UK-based office staff (currently between 220-240 employees per month); and
2. **FIELD**: Includes staff deployed internationally including to other MSF entities (currently between 60-80 employees per month).

In 2025, the total number of employees employed by MSF UK was 425 (avg. 301).

Currently, the People Services team manage delivery of both payrolls every month. Within this team, a People Services Officer is responsible for preparing both payrolls. They will be a key contact for this consultancy and their availability is impacted by payroll deadlines. The People Systems and Data Manager can step in to prepare both payrolls when required (in cases of absence, emergency etc.), although this is not part of the agreed scope of their role.

**Objectives**

The primary objective of the consultancy is to undertake an independent review of MSF UK’s end-to-end payroll function and provide recommendations for a future operating model.

**Scope of Work**

The review should cover:

- **Current operating model**: Review the existing payroll structure, including roles, responsibilities, ownership, internal capability and interaction with the outsourced payroll provider. Assess whether roles, responsibilities and ownership are appropriately defined and positioned within the organisation;
- **Processes and controls**: Review key payroll processes, controls, approvals and assurance arrangements, identifying gaps, inefficiencies, duplication, manual intervention and areas of risk;
- **Systems and technology**: Review MSF UK’s current systems, including interfaces, data flows and opportunities for greater automation and efficiency;
- **Risk and resilience**: Review business continuity, key-person dependencies, single points of failure, compliance considerations and the overall resilience of the current operating model;
- **Future requirements**: Consider MSF UK’s expected growth and future needs, including the appropriate capability, resourcing, organisational positioning and balance between internal and outsourced provision;
- **Future operating model**: Develop and evaluate options for the future operating model and recommend a preferred approach based on evidence. Assess whether current outsourced payroll provider is fit for purpose within the future operating model; and
- **Implementation plan**: Produce an end-to-end plan for implementation of the recommended future operating model, including immediate actions alongside longer term improvements and requirements for (if deemed to be required) an outsourced payroll provider.

The review should be informed by relevant good practice and appropriate external benchmarking where this adds value.

The following points are out of scope for this consultancy:

- Processing of monthly payrolls;
- Management, review or approval of monthly payrolls;
- Management of any staff members; and
- Procurement / Tendering of a new outsourced payroll provider (if required).

**Deliverables**

The consultant will be expected to provide the following deliverables:

- A report reviewing MSF UK’s current payroll function, highlighting key findings, risks, inefficiencies and areas for improvement;
- An options appraisal for the future delivery of payroll covering organisational positioning, resourcing, systems and the appropriate balance between internal and outsourced provision;
- PowerPoint presentations of findings and recommendations to key stakeholders to explain and explore the insights that have been identified to date;
- A report detailing the recommended future operating model supported by evidence and good practice followed by an updated version of the report detailing the recommended future operating model following collaboration and feedback from key stakeholders (following several rounds of discussion with key stakeholders in order to reach alignment);
- An implementation roadmap, identifying immediate and long-term actions as well as key considerations;
- A business case (following the MSF UK format) capturing the activities of the implementation roadmap. The business case should contain the rationale, options, indicative timing, associated costs, investment appraisal and risks of the activities; and
- A final PowerPoint presentation of findings, the recommended future operating model and implementation plan to key stakeholders (including senior management).

**Consultant Profile**

**Essential**

The successful consultant will be able to demonstrate:

- They are comfortable working autonomously and delivering with minimal supervision;
- Significant payroll expertise, including experience of reviewing and improving end-to-end payroll operations;
- Proven experience of payroll transformation, operating model reviews and/or the design and implementation of improved payroll arrangements;
- Strong knowledge of payroll governance, controls, compliance, risk management and business continuity;
- Experience of assessing payroll systems, processes and outsourced payroll arrangements, including opportunities for automation and process improvement;
- Experience of internal, outsourced and hybrid operating models;
- Ability to evaluate organisational structures, roles, responsibilities and resource requirements and translate findings into practical recommendations;
- Strong analytical and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to present complex findings and recommendations clearly to senior stakeholders; and
- Up to date knowledge of UK payroll legislation and recognised good practice.

**Desirable**

- Relevant professional payroll qualification;
- Experience undertaking similar independent payroll reviews within organisations of comparable scale or complexity;
- Experience of payroll transformation within a growing or changing organisation;
- Experience working across People/HR and Finance functions and advising on appropriate payroll ownership and governance; and
- Experience of selecting suitable outsourced payroll providers.

**Timescale and Key Milestones**

The consultancy is expected to commence on **8th January 2027** and complete all deliverables within **8 weeks**.

The consultant will be expected to agree to a detailed delivery plan at the outset of the assignment, including key milestones for stakeholder engagement, completion of the review, presentation of initial findings and delivery of final recommendations.

The proposed approach and timescale should ensure sufficient engagement with key stakeholders while maintaining momentum towards delivery of the final recommendations.

**Governance and Stakeholder Engagement**

The consultant will work closely with key stakeholders within MSF UK including People Services, Finance and relevant operational and system stakeholders, as well as the outsourced payroll provider where appropriate.

The consultant will report to the **Financial Controller** with regular progress updates agreed at the outset of the assignment. Key findings and recommendations should be appropriately tested with relevant stakeholders while maintaining the independence of the review.

**Confidentiality and Data Protection**

The consultant will be required to maintain appropriate confidentiality and comply with MSF UK’s data protection and information security requirements.

Given the sensitive nature of payroll information, any access to employee or payroll data must be limited to what is necessary to undertake the review and handled securely in accordance with UK GDPR and organisational policies.

**Commercial and Proposal Requirements**

Consultants should provide a proposal setting out:

- Their proposed approach and methodology for delivering the assignment;
- Relevant experience and examples of comparable payroll review or transformation work;
- Proposed timescale and availability;
- Proposed draft contract terms for supply of the service
- Details of the consultant(s) who will undertake the work; and
- A clear breakdown of fees and any associated costs.

The appointment will be subject to MSF UK standard procurement contracting and compliance requirements. The consultancy is intended to be engaged on an **outside IR35 basis**, subject to the appropriate status determination and contractual arrangements.

## **COOPI is looking for a Country Logistics Coordinator in Sudan**

**COOPI has been operating in Sudan since 2004**, providing humanitarian assistance and strengthening the resilience of populations affected by conflict, displacement, and climate-related shocks. Through an integrated, multisectoral approach, COOPI implements interventions in Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH), Food Security and Livelihoods (FSL), Shelter and Non-Food Items (NFI), Protection, and Cash-Based Assistance. Its programmes target the most vulnerable populations, including internally displaced persons (IDPs), returnees, refugees, and host communities, with particular attention to women, children, female-headed households, older persons, and persons with disabilities.

Following the outbreak of the conflict on 15 April 2023, COOPI significantly strengthened its emergency response to address the rapidly growing humanitarian needs. With its country coordination recently moved from Port Sudan to Khartoum, COOPI currently operates in North Darfur, and Khartoum, providing life-saving assistance, improving access to essential services, supporting livelihood recovery, and strengthening community-based protection mechanisms.

**Across its interventions, COOPI mainstreams Protection, Gender, Disability Inclusion, Disaster Risk Reduction (DRR), Climate Adaptation, and Accountability to Affected Populations (AAP). Its approach combines immediate humanitarian assistance with early recovery and resilience-building initiatives, promoting community participation, local partnerships, and sustainable solutions.**

COOPI implements its humanitarian programmes in collaboration with local and international partners, with support from **institutional donors, including the Italian Agency for Development Cooperation (AICS), the European Union, and United Nations agencies.**

## **Description of the role**

At the mission level, **the Country Logistics Coordinator, together with his dedicated team, is responsible for all logistics and security activities in the country** and plays a supporting, advisory and monitoring role vis-à-vis the project leaders and logistics assistants in the provinces to ensure that COOPI procedures and rules on procurement, logistics, document storage, equipment management and asset storage, and risk management (security, health, fraud) are known, understood and adhered to. **Capacity building is an essential aspect of the position.**

## **Responsibilities**

**Ensuring the logistical coordination of the country**

- **Carry out, follow-up or validate (as appropriate) the procurement procedures, supply of goods/services, transport systems and technical organisation of the work of all COOPI field offices in the country, as defined by the procedures;**
- **Ensure the practical and administrative management of the warehouses and inventory** of COOPI-owned and project-purchased durable goods;
- Coordinate the country logistics function at the national coordination level and provide **monitoring/support to the operational bases;**
- **Ensure the planning and organisation** of all logistical activities at national level;
- Ensure the proper functioning of logistics in terms of quality and quantity, at all different levels and in compliance with procedures;
- Ensure that **price/supplier databases** are up-to-date and regularly compared with those of similar organisations;
- Provide up-to-date costs and an up-to-date situation of the vehicle and equipment fleet for the preparation of new budgets, at the request of the Country Coordinator;

**Ensuring the application and implementation of procurement and procedures**

- **Ensure at national level the correct application of procedures - COOPI and donors** - concerning the procurement of goods and services (including planning and necessary documentation);
- **Monitor supplier performance** in accordance with COOPI standards;
- Properly monitor the delivery and archiving of all documentation produced in the process of acquiring goods/services, both nationally and internationally;
- **Verify and participate, if necessary, in the selection process of suppliers of goods and services and in the awarding of contracts for tenders and framework contracts**, with the aim of transparency and consistency;
- Provide adequate support concerning the elaboration of the **project procurement plan;**
- Use the **COOPI software** to monitor the management and archiving of document scans that constitute the procedures in the software.

**Coordinating and managing the vehicle fleet**

- For coordination, ensure the **administrative and operational management of the vehicle fleet, machines and electric generators;**
- In the field, monitor and control the correct administrative and operational management of the vehicle fleet, machines and electric generators, both through **monthly logistical reports and field visits;**
- Based on monthly logistical reports or through field inspections, check that the maintenance and repair of vehicles are carried out correctly and that monitoring reports and periodic checks are carried out correctly;
- **Ensure compliance** with the organisation's procedures and country regulations with regard to the use of vehicles and machines.

**Asset management**

- It is responsible for **monitoring durable goods** in coordination through the use of the tools provided by the COOPI Logistics Manual;
- Ensure the monitoring of durable goods on the ground using the tools provided by the COOPI logistics manual;
- Ensure that durable goods are properly maintained and that there are rules for the proper use of goods in the country;
- Carry out regular updates of the coordination and field project inventories: verify through the analysis of equipment monitoring or during field visits.

**Stock management**

- Carry out - and ensure based on - the **monitoring of storage warehouses and the use of reporting tools** by analysing stock monitoring in the monthly logistics report or during field visits;
- **Ensure that all goods are included in the inventory** and checked on entry and exit from the storage space according to COOPI procedures, as well as their identification and allocation to COOPI or a specific project.

**Computer systems management and maintenance**

- In coordination, ensure the management of the installation, maintenance and surveillance of **IT, communications, radio (HF, VHF) and satellite equipment;**
- **Provide adequate training** and support to personnel regarding the procedures for using the above-mentioned equipment.

**Security**

- It is responsible for **managing the security of the organisation's assets and property;**
- **Collaborate and assist the Country Coordinator / Project Managers** on security aspects of expatriate and local staff;
- Develop and implement a specific **security plan and Standard Operating Procedures (SoP)** for the bases of competence;
- Provide assistance and support for the development of security plans and Standard Operating Procedures (SoP) of the bases;
- Contribute to the collection of security information and implement preventive actions and mechanisms;
- **Ensure the dissemination of security procedures** by scheduling briefings with new staff and regular updates with all staff;
- In coordination and at the bases, in cooperation with personnel on the ground, ensure adequate security conditions for the organisation's assets and property.

**Ensuring personnel management**

- **Coordinate, supervise and evaluate personnel** in hierarchical relation;
- **Organise the training of logistics staff** working on country projects (identification of team weaknesses and targeted training);
- Ensuring compliance with residency and/or entry authorisation procedures in the country;
- Ensuring COOPI staff are welcomed at the airport and arranging accommodation for expatriate staff.

**Ensuring the reporting and documentation of logistics**

- Ensuring that all reports related to the logistics sector are produced as stipulated by COOPI procedures and the Country Rules (vehicle and goods management, supplies, purchasing, warehousing, personnel reports, etc.).

**Institutional relations**

- On behalf of the Country Coordinator, represent the organisation during logistics cluster meetings and security briefings (INSO, UNDSS, Embassies, etc.).

## **Requirements**

**ESSENTIAL**

- **Degree in Logistics or higher education or LLA (Logistics Learning Alliance) certification in Humanitarian Logistics;**
- **Working experience in humanitarian logistics of at least 5 years;**
- **Previous experience in logistics coordination of at least 1 to 2 years** in similar organizations (in terms of size and constraints);
- Indispensable knowledge of the **English language**;
- Good command of common software;
- Strong organizational skills;
- Willingness to motivate and accompany teams in emergency contexts;
- **Good physical condition and ability to travel and stay in remote or unsafe areas;**
- Excellent negotiation and communication skills;
- Result orientation, drive for improvement, creativity, initiative;
- **Interest in training, supporting and consulting multicultural teams;**
- Ability to work under pressure and autonomously.

**DESIRABLE**

- Experience with COOPI and knowledge of its logistics procedures.;
- **Previous experience in Sudan.**

We reserve the right to close the vacancy prior to the application closing date, once a sufficient number of applications has been received. **Please note that only the shortlisted candidates will be contacted.**

*COOPI Cooperazione Internazionale is an Italian secular and independent humanitarian organisation fighting against all forms of poverty to improve the world. Founded in 1965, today COOPI is present in 27 countries in Africa, Latin America and the Caribbean and the Middle East, with both emergency and development projects carried out by expatriate staff – an average of over 200 people per year – and national staff. The main institutional funders are the European Union – ECHO and INTPA – USAID, UN agencies, the Italian Government, local authorities and other European Governments.*

The International Rescue Committee (IRC) responds to the world's worst humanitarian crises, helping to restore health, safety, education, economic wellbeing, and power to people devastated by conflict and disaster. Founded in 1933 at the call of Albert Einstein, the IRC is one of the world's largest international humanitarian non-governmental organizations (INGO), at work in more than 40 countries and 29 U.S. cities helping people to survive, reclaim control of their future and strengthen their communities. A force for humanity, IRC employees deliver lasting impact by restoring safety, dignity and hope to millions. If you're a solutions-driven, passionate change-maker, come join us in positively impacting the lives of millions of people world-wide for a better future.

**Job Background / Overview:**

The External Relations (ER) department includes private fundraising, marketing & communications and advocacy. ER’s goal is to mobilize external audiences to raise funding to meet core organization needs deliver programs that support our clients to recover and gain control of their lives; influence policy changes to improve outcomes for our clients; and build and grow our global brand.

The Officer, Prospect Research & Management, sits within External Relations’ Prospect Research & Management team, supporting the IRC’s sustainability by finding opportunities for funding, focusing our resources, and protecting our reputation through due diligence. This is a 12-month fixed-term position. They are responsible for rigorously synthesizing data and information sources, providing senior staff and frontline fundraisers with high-quality research and actionable insights on US-based donors, as well as strategic feedback for board meetings, donor meetings, and events. They manage portfolio relationships with Officer-level fundraisers, delivering to agreed strategies and influencing decision-making through research-derived insights. The right candidate will be comfortable producing core prospect research products, and will build positive relationships across internal teams and external vendors, while delivering superior research and reporting services that ultimately increase philanthropic support for our clients.

**Major Responsibilities:**

- Deliver high-quality confidential biographical, financial, and due diligence research profiles on US-based HNWI donors and prospects within agreed deadlines and parameters, drawing on a range of online and electronic sources;
- Build robust pipelines for fundraisers focused on US market by identifying, rating, and qualifying HNWI donors capable of significant financial support;
- Manage portfolio relationships with Officer-level frontline fundraising colleagues in the US, delivering to agreed strategies and developing business-oriented recommendations for donors and prospects, with the broader goal to increase support for the IRC’s clients;
- Translate research into actionable insights, integrating data-driven and qualitative analyses and broader trends into tactics and strategies for prospect/donor management, opportunity development, and maximized revenue;
- Contribute ideas to improve tools and methods that support comprehensive prospect research and portfolio management, including relationship mapping, analytics, and other appropriate metrics;
- Manage a suite of resources (e.g. CRM, subscription databases, media tools) to understand and respond to specific opportunities;
- Contribute to special projects (e.g. vendor screenings, data mining, ad hoc reports, etc.) and conduct proactive research projects in partnership with colleagues across the organization, identifying new opportunities that align with stakeholder teams’ strategies;
- Collaborate with prospect research-adjacent teams (Philanthropy account managers, Program Services team) to ensure a smooth and efficient overall service to IRC’s high value fundraising teams, actively seeking and incorporating stakeholder feedback;
- Follow team best practice and process guidance, and produce documentation to support team protocols;
- Work with line manager to prioritize a varied workload (research profiles, reports, ad hoc analysis), adapting to changing stakeholder priorities while meeting established timelines

**Essential Qualifications:**

- Bachelor’s degree required; Master’s degree preferred
- 3–5 years’ experience applying quantitative and qualitative analysis and business intelligence in support of non-profit fundraising, with familiarity with US markets;
- High proficiency in internet search engines and sophisticated search techniques, fundraising software, data retrieval services (e.g. LexisNexis, iWave, Foundation Directory, etc.), MS Office (Word, Outlook and Excel, in particular).
- Experience working with a relational database is essential, preferably Salesforce

**Demonstrated Skills and Competencies:**

- Research/database management (Salesforce);
- Analyzing, interpreting, and synthesizing data, and translating findings into actionable recommendations;
- Ability to apply and contribute to proactive prospecting methodologies that support organizational goals, including data mining and sophisticated constituent database queries;
- Excellent verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to explain complex research clearly, concisely, and in an actionable way

**Desired Qualifications:**

- Keen understanding of philanthropic trends, in particular the impacts on humanitarian aid sector
- Knowledge and curiosity about emerging concepts and practices in the field of strategic philanthropy, corporate social responsibility, Public Private Partnerships, and impact investing, particularly as it relates to U.S. entities;
- Strong interpersonal and time-management skills; confirmed ability to prioritize and deliver on time;
- Ability to act with sensitivity and discretion while working with highly confidential information;
- Prior experience working at an international organization outside the US.

**Working Environment:**

This role may require working remotely full- or part-time (i.e., telecommuting). Applicants must have a home or alternate workspace they can effectively complete their work from during regularly scheduled hours.

At the IRC, we seek to build a welcoming, inclusive environment not only for our refugee clients, but for all of our employees. As an equal opportunity employer, we value the strength found in the diversity of voices, experiences, and backgrounds who join us in our work. Fostering an inclusive culture with equitable practices is everyone's responsibility, and we regularly offer trainings and opportunities to learn more and find ways to do better. We do not discriminate on the basis of race, religion, color, national origin, sex, gender, gender expression, sexual orientation, age, marital status, veteran status, socioeconomic status, or disability status. We are dedicated to ensuring that disabled individuals are provided reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and to receive other benefits and privileges of employment. Please contact us to request accommodation. In your application, please feel free to indicate your preferred pronouns.

We recognize that few applicants may "check all the boxes," but that each person has their own unique strengths that they would bring to the table. In our commitment to building a diverse and authentic work culture, we invite you to apply anyway. You belong here.

**Compensation:** ($72,040 - $81,000/annually). Ranges are based on various factors including the labor market, job type, internal equity, and budget. Exact offers are calibrated by work location, individual candidate experience and skills relative to the defined job requirements.

**Professional Standards:** All International Rescue Committee workers must adhere to the core values and principles outlined in [IRC Way - Standards for Professional Conduct](https://www.rescue.org/page/our-code-conduct). Our Standards are Integrity, Service, Equality and Accountability. In accordance with these values, the IRC operates and enforces policies on [Safeguarding](https://www.rescue.org/page/safeguarding), Conflicts of Interest, Fiscal Integrity, and Reporting Wrongdoing and Protection from Retaliation. IRC is committed to take all necessary preventive measures and create an environment where people feel safe, and to take all necessary actions and corrective measures when harm occurs. IRC builds teams of professionals who promote critical reflection, power sharing, debate, and objectivity to deliver the best possible services to our clients.

**Benefits:** IRC offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package. Highlights include 10 paid sick leave, 10 U.S. holidays, 20-25 generous paid time off based on role and tenure, medical insurance starting at $163/month, dental starting at $6.50/month, and vision starting at $5/month, flexible spending accounts (healthcare, dependent care, and commuter), a 403(b) retirement savings plan with employer match (immediate vesting) along with a non-elective employer contribution based on age and years of service (3-year vesting), disability and life insurance, and an Employee Assistance Program (EAP) offering confidential counseling and support services for employees and their families.

